MySQL is a heavily guarded bank vault with one head teller who verifies every single ledger entry so the numbers are always 100% exact.
Apache Cassandra is a global network of thousands of independent drop-boxes with no single boss—you choose Cassandra over MySQL the moment your 
business needs massive, nonstop write throughput, linear horizontal scale, and zero-downtime global availability, and you are willing to trade away complex
SQL joins to get it.

Technical Breakdown :- 

Write Architecture (Masterless LSM-Tree vs. Primary B-Tree): MySQL uses a Primary-Replica model backed by B-Trees. 
Every write funnels through a single Primary node, creating a hard write bottleneck and a failover delay if that node dies.
Cassandra is completely masterless (peer-to-peer ring) and uses LSM-Trees (Log-Structured Merge-Trees), appending writes sequentially to a 
Commit Log and in-memory Memtable. Every node can accept writes simultaneously, so doubling your nodes literally doubles your write throughput.

CAP Theorem & Consistency: MySQL prioritizes ACID compliance and strong consistency across multi-table transactions. 
Cassandra is an AP (Availability + Partition Tolerance) system with Tunable Consistency—you can configure reads and writes
per query (e.g., ONE for raw speed, or QUORUM for strong consistency) so the database stays online even if an entire data center goes dark.

Query Flexibility: MySQL excels at normalized schemas and ad-hoc JOINs. Cassandra forbids joins and requires you to denormalize data into wide-column tables
designed around exact primary-key lookups.

Buisness Context :-
You choose Cassandra for write-heavy, mission-critical, globally distributed workloads with predictable access patterns—like ingesting millions 
of IoT sensor pings per second, storing chat history for a global messaging app, or serving real-time clickstream/recommendation feeds where downtime costs 
millions per minute. You stick with MySQL for core transactional systems—like banking ledgers, e-commerce checkout, or HR/ERP systems—where strict referential
integrity, multi-row ACID transactions, and unpredictable reporting queries are non-negotiable.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers often migrate to Cassandra just to escape MySQL scaling pain, then try to run ad-hoc queries with ALLOW FILTERING—which forces 
a full-cluster scan and instantly crashes production. As a data engineer, I enforce Query-First Data Modeling: in Cassandra, you design one denormalized 
table per specific read query, picking a high-cardinality Partition Key to prevent hot nodes and Clustering Columns to sort range scans on disk. 
I also strictly avoid Cassandra for update- or delete-heavy workloads: because it is append-only, deletes write 'Tombstone' markers that accumulate on 
disk and severely degrade read latency if not carefully managed through compaction.
